The WFS, current, voltage and arc power captured during the build process were plotted with respect to layer number for further analysis and are shown in Fig.  4  and Table  2 . The plot represents slight fluctuation in the value, which can be attributed to the extraneous variables as well as the variation in standoff distance along the build height. The magnitude of current (106 A), voltage (14.2 V) and arc power (2.082 kW) showed elevated levels for the WFS and TS (3, 80) in contrast to other feasible variables, potentially due to the presence of a larger WFS [ 40 ]. However, it is imperative to acknowledge that the measured WFS differs from programmed WFS. A similar observation was also made by Wang et al. [ 41 ]. The fundamental operation of CMT for one cycle of the process encompasses several stages, including the short circuit, pulse and wait phases, each characterised by distinct current values [ 42 ]. Consequently, this variation in current values leads to varying WFS, resulting in a mean WFS that deviates from the input value. Additionally, the TS values of 60, 70 and 80 cm/min exhibit negligible differences in current, voltage and arc power indicating the lack of dependence. Besides, the HI values in kJ/cm were calculated using the formulae: 1 HI = η × V × I × 60 T S × 1000 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
